<section class="page-hero" style="padding:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="inner">
      <div class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg> <span>Compare</span></div>
      <h1 style="margin-top:10px">Three evaluations, thirty-three products, one rubric</h1>
      <p class="lede">We asked AI to evaluate the badminton club-night market against a rubric written before anything was scored, for one kind of club: a UK social night, 18 to 26 players, four courts, mixed strength, where the live night is the main problem. We ran it three times on the same day, across two vendors and three models. <strong>ePegboard came first in all three.</strong> It scored 90.5, then 95.5, then 70.5 - and that spread is the more useful half of the result.</p>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span class="gm-tag">Evaluation</span>
        <span>3 runs</span>
        <span>33 products</span>
        <span>12 September 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:900px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>One evaluation tells you almost nothing.</strong> You cannot separate a real result from a lucky prompt, a generous model or a bad afternoon's web crawling. Three let you see which findings hold and which move - and on this evidence, exactly one does.</p>

      <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> we wrote the rubric and ran these evaluations ourselves, and we sell one of the products in them. Nobody independent commissioned this. The runs were Claude Opus 5 and two ChatGPT models, in September 2026. Every prompt and every unedited response is published below, including the run that scores us lowest and the section explaining what most of our lead actually measures.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:28px">
      <h4>On this page</h4>
      <a href="#runs">The three runs</a>
      <a href="#holds">What holds, and what does not</a>
      <a href="#table">Every product, every run</a>
      <a href="#margin">What our lead actually measures</a>
      <a href="#weak">Where we are weak</a>
      <a href="#others">When another product is the better answer</a>
      <a href="#wrong">What we got wrong, and what we fixed</a>
      <a href="#practice">Whether clubs actually stay</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="runs">The three runs</h2>

      <p>Same fifteen criteria, same weights, same club profile, same candidate list, same day. The only thing that changed between them was the model doing the reading.</p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>Retrieval</th><th>Field</th><th>ePegboard</th><th>Second place</th><th>Margin</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in runs)
            {
              <tr>
                <td>@r.Model</td><td>@r.Retrieval</td><td>@r.Field</td>
                <td><strong>@r.Us</strong></td><td>@r.Second</td><td>@r.Margin</td>
              </tr>
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="holds">What holds, and what does not</h2>

      <p><strong>One finding survives all three runs: ePegboard comes first out of 33 products.</strong> Two vendors, three models, identical inputs.</p>

      <p><strong>The score does not survive at all.</strong> It is 90.5, 95.5 and 70.5 - <strong>a twenty-point spread from the same inputs on the same day</strong>, with nothing changed but which model was reading. That is a measure of how harshly a given evaluator reads a documentation page, not of how well a product runs a Tuesday night. Anyone quoting a single number off a comparison like this, ours included, is quoting the evaluator rather than the product.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>If you take one number off this page, take the margin, not the score</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Our lead over second place is <strong>6.0, 8.5 and 10.0</strong> points. Always positive, never comfortable - and <em>smallest</em> in the run that read the field most completely.</p>
      </div>

      <p><strong>Second place is not stable either.</strong> ShuttlSync takes it in two runs; ShuttleFlow and Racket Social tie for it in the third, where ShuttlSync drops to eighth. Read the table below and you will find products that move forty points between runs. Below first place this is not a ranking - it is noise with a leader sitting on top of it, and a club should shortlist two or three and try them.</p>

      <h2 id="table">Every product, every run</h2>

      <p><strong>Every figure is a range:</strong> the lower bound is what that run could evidence, the upper bound is what the product would reach if every criterion it could not establish turned out to be fully met. A single figure means nothing was left unresolved. A dash means that run resolved nothing for that product - <strong>never zero, and never a judgement on it.</strong></p>

      

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Product</th><th>Claude Opus 5</th><th>ChatGPT (free)</th><th>ChatGPT 5.6</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in rows)
            {
              <tr>
                <td>@(r.Product == "ePegboard" ? Html.Raw("<strong>ePegboard</strong>") : Html.Raw(r.Product))</td>
                <td>@r.Opus</td>
                <td>@r.Free</td>
                <td>@r.Sol</td>
              </tr>
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Read across the rows, not down the columns.</strong> ShuttleFlow is 55.0 in one run and 85.5 in another. Racket Social 77.5 and 85.5 and 50.0. eBadders 67.0 and 48.0 and a range that spans almost the whole scale. The disagreement is the honest finding, and any comparison claiming a confident ordering of thirty-three products is selling you something.</p>

      <p><strong>A note on Court Buddy.</strong> It scores well, and the score is honest - its documented sit-out fairness is among the clearest in the field. But its website is a pickleball session manager and never mentions badminton, though its app listing does name badminton club nights. We asked every run to record what sport each product presents itself as being for, precisely so this is visible rather than buried.</p>

      <p><strong>And one product in the field does not exist.</strong> We invented a name and put it in the candidate list as a check. <strong>No run scored it</strong> - all three reported it unlocatable. That control exists because the previous set had one that failed in the opposite direction: we listed a real product, could not find it ourselves, and concluded it was fictional.</p>

      <h2 id="margin">What our lead actually measures</h2>

      <p>This is the part we would leave out if we were only selling. It is the evaluator's own conclusion, in its own words, from the run that read the field most completely:</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <p style="margin-top:0"><em>"How much of ePegboard's six-point lead is documentation depth rather than demonstrated capability? <strong>Most of it.</strong> A product whose picker behaves identically but whose site carries six feature bullets would score 12 to 18 points lower on identical behaviour. That is a property of the rubric... The honest reading is that ePegboard's lead is <strong>evidence of seriousness, not proof of superiority.</strong>"</em></p>
      </div>

      <p>The rubric awards full marks only where a vendor describes how something behaves - the rule, the edge cases, the limits. That is a documentation test by construction, and we publish several hundred pages of documentation. A product that does the same things without writing them down scores lower, and deserves not to be dismissed on that basis.</p>

      <p>The same run puts the point harder: of thirty-two scored products, <strong>exactly one had any capability demonstrated rather than described</strong> - and only because it publishes nothing to describe, so the evaluator ran it instead. Every other score on this page, ours first, is a vendor's account of its own product.</p>

      <p><strong>Where the ordering should not be trusted at all:</strong> that run puts ePegboard, ShuttlSync, ShuttlePilot and vsBadminton inside the noise of each other, and notes that <strong>ShuttlePilot and vsBadminton document late arrivals and odd numbers better than we do</strong> - and would likely overtake us if the rubric weighted that more heavily. We think that is worth knowing before you take our first place at face value.</p>

      <h2 id="weak">Where we are weak</h2>

      <p>From the runs' own assessments of the leader:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Payments are a record, not a till.</strong> We log what was collected. Cash, cheque or transfer, entered afterwards, one attendee at a time - no part-payment ledger, no mark-everyone-paid. Your treasurer still does the collecting.</li>
        <li><strong>Offline costs money and runs on Windows.</strong> The web app needs a connection. Fully offline means our separate desktop product at £60 a year, and new features land on the web app first. If your hall has no signal, the free product is not the one you need.</li>
        <li><strong>Competitions are summarised, not documented</strong>, and are handicap doubles only - singles and team formats are not built.</li>
        <li><strong>It is a tablet-and-laptop product.</strong> We recommend against running the night from a phone. A phone-only organiser is better served by ShuttlePilot.</li>
        <li><strong>Everything here is our word.</strong> Not one of our full marks was demonstrated to anybody.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="others">When another product is the better answer</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A hall with no reliable connection: ShuttlSync or Racket Social.</strong> Both do real offline operation at no cost - roster prepared online, session run offline, synced after. Ours is a paid Windows product.</li>
        <li><strong>Collecting the money rather than recording it: ShuttleStats or ShuttleOps.</strong> ShuttleStats puts the club on its own Stripe account, charges per payment with no subscription, and handles capacity, waiting lists and refunds.</li>
        <li><strong>Running the whole night one-handed from a phone: ShuttlePilot.</strong> Built around the fact that the person running club night is usually also holding a racket.</li>
        <li><strong>Leagues and a membership register: ServeLeague or SportyHQ.</strong> Six documented league formats and a constitutionally sound member register, against our handicap doubles.</li>
        <li><strong>Printing the whole evening in advance: SportRevive.</strong> A different model to live picking, and for a stable group of nine it is less fuss than any queue.</li>
        <li><strong>A rating players take seriously: UBR or Ladderly.</strong> Both publish their maths in more depth than we publish ours.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="wrong">What we got wrong, and what we fixed</h2>

      <p>The first three runs produced a defensible result on an indefensible set of inputs. Rather than publish the caveats, we fixed the inputs and ran it again. Everything below was wrong, and is now corrected in the runs above.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>We told ourselves a real competitor did not exist.</strong> SmashHQ was in the list from an old note with no address. Two of our searches found nothing, so we recorded it as fictional - and treated an earlier run that <em>had</em> found it as inventing evidence. It is a live product. It now scores in all three current runs.</li>
        <li><strong>We pointed the entire exercise at the wrong page for Court Manager</strong> - its admin login rather than its product site - and then published that it had no product information. Given the right address it places sixth, and the evaluator calls its picker documentation the most complete configuration description of any candidate in the field.</li>
        <li><strong>Six sites we reported as unreadable were working normally.</strong> They build their pages in the browser and our reader could not run JavaScript. That was our method failing, described as their websites failing.</li>
        <li><strong>Three real rivals were missing from the field</strong> - ShuttlSync, ShuttleStats and SportRevive - because research done for our own guide never reached the list we drew candidates from. ShuttlSync now places second in two of the three current runs.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The pattern in all four is the same, and worth naming: <strong>our tooling could not see something, and we wrote that down as a fact about somebody else's product.</strong></p>

      <h2 id="practice">Whether clubs actually stay</h2>

      <p>A documentation score measures what a product says about itself. The question underneath it is whether clubs run a second night:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Two in three</strong> clubs that ran a real first session with ePegboard this year ran another.</li>
        <li><strong>Getting on for half</strong> have run five or more.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Measured on 9 September 2026, across clubs whose first session was in 2026, excluding demo and template clubs. A "session" means one where a game was actually started - counting every session record merely created would give four in five and nearly two in three, and we would rather publish the figure that survives the stricter reading.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>The test that beats all three of these</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Run your own awkward night: a late arrival after three games, someone sitting out and rejoining, two courts finishing together, a pairing you want kept, a suggestion you overrule, and a wrong score that needs correcting. Then ask the organiser whether it took decisions out of their head or just moved them onto a screen.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:14px">ePegboard is free for clubs. So are several of the products above. Try more than one.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:14px"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://play.epegboard.com/instant-demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the instant demo</a> &nbsp; <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://play.epegboard.com/signup" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Set up a free club</a></p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">Method and sources</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">All three runs used the same pre-declared 100-point rubric with weights unaltered. The criteria and weights, the prompt, the evidence rules, each run's corrections and retrieval logs and all three unedited responses are on the <a href="/compare/method">method page</a> - The four things we got wrong in an earlier attempt, and fixed before these runs, are listed there too. Product pages were read in September 2026 and products change; verify anything material with the supplier. For a shorter read on which tool suits which club, see <a href="/guides/best-badminton-club-night-software">our club-night software comparison</a>.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
